`src/ai_insar/insardev_optional.py` calls public entry points only when
that install is present:

- Interferograms call `Stack.pairs().interferogram()` first, with no
  filter wavelength (single-look `reference * conjugate(repeat)`). The
  later date is the reference. A returned complex cube is kept even when
  its values differ from the NumPy/Numba product in
  `src/ai_insar/pipeline/interferogram.py`. That in-tree product runs
  when `insardev` is missing, the call fails, or `AI_INSAR_BACKEND=numpy`.
- `Stack.load` is tried first for scene paths, including SAFE zip lists.
  A returned complex cube is the stack. A failed call on zip paths uses
  this project's crop reader. Upstream `Stack.load` asks for a dask
  client, so a zip list often falls back after that failure.
- `Stack.align` is tried when the loaded object has that method.
  `AI_INSAR_BACKEND=numpy` skips `Stack.load` and `Stack.align`.

InSARdev/core `insardev_pygmtsar` and `insardev_toolkit` (BSD-3-Clause)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Copyright (c) 2025, Alexey Pechnikov
https://github.com/InSARdev/core

BSD-3-Clause would allow a copy that keeps the copyright notice, the
license conditions, and the disclaimer, and that does not use the author's
name to endorse this project. Those packages are still not vendored. The
`insardev` extra pulls `insardev_toolkit` because upstream declares that
dependency. `insardev_pygmtsar` is not part of the extra.

`insardev_toolkit.PyGMTSAR.safeload` translates SAFE burst products into
the InSARdev layout when you pass burst ids. `insardev_pygmtsar.S1.align_ref`
and `align_rep` are geometric coregistration and need a burst directory,
orbits, and a DEM. The wrappers call those functions when the packages
import and you pass those inputs. The SAFE zip crop does not call them
and does not coregister the window. They scan GMTSAR-style burst
directories. They do not replace the complex64 crop reader.

